title The pointer with no claim -- two ships widened one guard in one hour
prompt autonomous loop, fire rota lap; the lane's own crux (%589) waits on Keaton's word, so claim-as-override took the oldest unclaimed booked lap
rota lap 4272, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; read the threshold page, then Standfast, reds-first, and the three depths. Fire's practice is read the ledger before reading anything new, and that is literally what sent this lap: %601's closing sentence named an unclaimed lap and I took it.
think My lane is mantra/ and tally/. %589, the Weave port, is mechanical now and stays Keaton's word, so the lane's agent-doable queue was empty and claim-as-override applies.
think The cold open refused: a roster pass from my previous lap still held the lock, its group leader gone, its output at /tmp/patchouli-cold-423382.log. I read that file rather than killing thirteen minutes of work, and resolved pid 423396 through /proc/<pid>/cwd before touching it -- never a pattern, per the baton.
think That pass closed tree_moved=yes with index_row_bound red. The cause was my own round-open reset landing at 06:19:11, mid-pass: the guard read the shelf as it stood before the reset adopted upstream's repaired one. I re-ran cold on the settled tree rather than publish a reading taken across a reset.
think Second cold pass: 187 guards, 1,445s, 182 green, 2 red, 3 gated, tree_moved=no. index_row_bound green, confirming the diagnosis. stash_record red at orphans=34 is %592's standing seat; standing_equipment reds while any do.
think %601 leaves as a lap of its own: a guard reading tracked runners for a fixed /tmp/<name> write. That guard exists -- tools/fixtures/s/shared_pen_scan.sh -- and its sources line read git ls-files 'tools/*', so 68 tracked .sh and .rish runners outside that room were never read.
think Measured before building: 68 files outside tools/, of which exactly one carries a constant pen -- rishi/tests/file_io.rish, three names, no wipe. Its list-dir "/tmp" assertions can pass on a peer's files with write-file broken, which is a false green rather than a clobber.
think Proven from the failing side in a pen: over a tree holding one runner outside tools/ with a constant pen, the elder scan answers constant_pen_files=0 verdict=ok and the widened one answers 1.
think Built the widening, the repair, three control legs and the witness assertions; scan read 3,294 sources with 46 files and 15 wiping unchanged; control 44 behaviors proven; witness GREEN.
obs Then the fetch before booking the REDS row showed xy had moved twice, and git diff --name-only HEAD xy/main over my own five paths named four of them. COPAL landed the identical work at 20260908.052557 as ac25bfffc0 -- same source line, same one file, same anchor room, same three control legs, the same 236/46/15 either side.
obs I dropped my four duplicate files and adopted upstream whole. Keeping a redundant diff to show effort would cost a reviewer a merge and teach nothing.
obs The one thing COPAL's lap left behind is real: tools/fixtures/rish_io/README.md still said the room held only the witness's scratch, while their repair had just moved a second writer into it. That is docs-implementation-sync, and it is this lap's only landed change to the tree besides the ledger and the card.
obs %619 books the collision. Seventeen firings of the claim question were two hands finding one fault; this one is sharper -- the ledger POINTED at the work, in a row's own closing sentence, and recorded nobody taking it. A pointer with no claim beside it is an invitation to duplicate, addressed to everyone.
obs The HOT pass read 8 red where the cold read 2, and six were the ledger family answering my own new row: reds_ledger_headline wanted its total moved 618 -> 619, reds_status_consistency read the phrase %601's closing clause as a claim to close %601 and named it contradicted, unshared_citation named the card citing %619 above the anointed spine's shared_max of 618, and dayshelf_merge simply reported that this clone had never armed BAKERY's new merge driver. Each named its own repair on its face; all seven re-verified GREEN. stash_record stays red, as it was at the cold open.
obs The card now cites the row by stamp rather than by number, which is derived-spine rule 4 doing its job on the first lap it could: %619 is unshared until xy binds it.
obs Named against the shelving gesture: fold_shelf_link_scan.sh reads git ls-files, which is the INDEX, so a brand-new untracked shelf is invisible to it. That is why every shelving lap reds once at the HOT pass and repairs once -- the fault is structural rather than a habit. I repaired my shelf's climbing link before staging, so the hot pass should not red there.
